when you think about Batman your mind  probably goes to the grim avenger who  stalks gotham city by night dishing out  a darker brand of justice and you're not  wrong that's the image that's made  Batman one of the most popular  superheroes to ever hit the comics page  and propelled him to success in TV and  movies as well since 1939 though there  have been thousands of stories about  Batman and sometimes things get pretty  weird from bizarre adventures to  long-lost relatives there are more than  a handful of things about Batman the DC  Comics would probably be happy for  readers to forget the dark knock off  after his debut in 1938 Superman's  sudden and overwhelming success prompted  a massive crowd of imitators but Batman  was different the darker costumed the  menacing demeanor and even the fact that  he didn't have superpowers helped him to  stand out in the crowd but unique isn't  really the right word for him Bill  Finger and Bob Kane were pretty much  just ripping off the shadow the first  Batman story the case of the chemical  syndicate appeared in Detective Comics  number 27 in 1939 but three years  earlier the shadow a pulp hero known for  his dark costume and menacing demeanor  starred in a novel called partners of  peril to say that Batman's first  adventure borrowed from that story is  the understatement of the century  it's a beat for beat adaptation right on  down to Batman's early pension for guns  and his identity being revealed as a  millionaire socialite the good news is  that within a few months finger and  artists like Jerry Robinson and Sheldon  mauled awfully separated the Caped  Crusader from his inspiration and hey at  least he's not alone just ask Superman  if he knows anything about Clark Doc  Savage and the Arctic Fortress of  Solitude he had in 1938 my brother my  Deadman and me way back in 1974 is  world's finest number 223 Bob Haney and  dick Dillon told a story so weird that  even Batman himself referred to it as  the most stunning shocking thing that  ever happened in my whole life which has  not exactly been dull in a team-up with  Superman and the ghostly dead man  Batman followed the trail of a murderer  who killed his victims with gigantic  razor-sharp boomerangs and discovered  that the man he was hunting was actually  the brother he never knew it seems his  big bro Thomas Wayne jr. had been driven  insane by a car accident from when he  was a baby  he learned how to make boomerangs as  part of his therapy and was then  elated into a very complicated murder  spree and that's not even the weird part  that happens at the end of the story  when dead man a ghostly superhero with  the ability to possess the living just  decides to take over Thomas's body as  his new flesh-and-blood home without  ever telling Batman instead he just  assumes that his buddy Batman would be  cool with it and wanders off to have his  own adventures  needless to say while we would  eventually see more of Thomas jr. no  other Creators ever referenced this  particular story again Alfred goes bad  speaking of weird things that have  happened to the Batman family  Alfred's brief tenure as a telekinetic  crime boss who looked like a humanoid  golf ball is also something that we  don't really talk about anymore in 1964  jack schiff and sheldon mauled off  seemingly killed off Batman's Butler  Alfred having him sacrifice his life to  save the dynamic duo  six months later though Batman faced a  new foe the outsider for almost two  years the outsider challenged Batman and  Robin acting as a mastermind who  manipulated other villains and when they  finally confronted him he was revealed  to be Alfred it turned out that their  faithful Butler had been resurrected by  a scientist whose regeneration machine  turned him into a mutant who hated his  former employers fortunately the process  turned out to be extremely easy to  reverse and before long he was back to  dusting the Batcave and believe it or  not this one actually did get referenced  again later on nocturne ha's custody  battle if you're a casual Batman fan you  might think that he only did weird stuff  back in the long-ago days of the Silver  Age once the 80s rolled around with Dark  Knight Returns and death and the family  things got dark and serious right sure  as long as dark and serious includes a  vampire taking Batman to court to get  custody of Robin it happened in 1983  with Natalya Knight who was about as  close as you could get to a vampire  without actually having someone get  bitten on the neck while working as an  astronomer she was zapped by a weird ray  that drained her skin of pigmentation  and left her super sensitive to light so  naturally she decided to become a super  villain named Nocturna like you do she  also fell into a love quadrangle with  Vicki Vale and Alfred's daughter Julia  who were all vying for Bruce Wayne's  affections the thing is rather than long  walks on the beach Nocturna strategy  involved waiting for Bruce to adopt a  new Ward Jason Todd  and then adopting him first so that  Bruce would have to marry her if he  really wanted to be a bad dad the  weirdest part it almost worked  Jason actually said in court that he'd  prefer to be adopted by Natalya only to  reveal later that it had all been part  of an ill-fated attempt at going  undercover  eventually Nocturna was seemingly killed  in a hot-air balloon crash no really and  that was the end of the world's most  complicated marriage proposal electric  death when you think about Batman's  greatest enemies the names that usually  come to mind are the big ones the Joker  Ra's al Ghul you can even make a pretty  decent argument for the Riddler if you  really put your mind to it but if you're  going by who actually had the most  success at the goal of actually killing  Batman then the Dark Knight's greatest  foe is actually a guy named Lester  Bachynski the electrocutioner yeah it's  hard to believe especially since he  technically killed Batman by accident  the electrocutioner was another  vigilante who figured that if you  couldn't bring the criminals to the  electric chair you might as well bring  the electric chair to them in a 1992  story from Detective Comics he and  Batman were on the trail of the same  criminal when the electrocutioner zapped  him with a shock big enough to stop his  heart  Batman was technically dead for a good  minute and a half the only reason that  he didn't stay that way was that Robin  was there and had the good sense to wear  an insulated costume while the  electrocutioner originally just shrugged  off literally killing the Batman Robin  eventually beat enough sense into him  that he agreed to use his electric  gloves as a defibrillator to shock  Batman back to life and then they both  presumably agreed to never again speak  of the time Batman almost died in a  sewer at the hands of a delish Club that  had to be beaten into compliance by a  spiky haired teenager with a stick the  dark knock off returns with Batman's  popularity it's no surprise that Gotham  City would become a pretty important  place in the DC Universe the last 75  years have seen a history developed for  the Dark Knight's hometown that's also  seen it serve as the home of other  superheroes from throughout the timeline  of the DC Universe the Golden Age Green  Lantern a violent vigilante called the  Reaper who was around in the years  before Bruce Wayne's parents were killed  and even cowboy antihero Jonah Hex who  was hanging around there back in the  1800s and also in the future  it's complicated of course it's one  thing if Batman's not the first  superhero to make his home in Gotham  City but if he's not actually the first  Gotham super  to dress up as a bat well that's a  little weird in 1998 an unexpected tale  in the pages of chase a short-lived but  underrated comic series revealed that  Cameron chases distrust of masked  Avengers came from the fact that her  father was one the Acrobat who fought  crime in the 70s alongside an obscure  and hilariously named team called the  Justice experience unsurprisingly the  story doesn't end well but once you know  he was out there it's hard not to think  that Bruce Wayne was biting his style  the corpse that wouldn't die the  electrocutioner wasn't the only one to  score a technical kill on Batman in the  brave in the bolt number 115 however the  culprit was even less impressive  a booby-trapped rain spout rigged up by  a couple of completely average criminals  the shock did him in but thanks to  Batman's iron Constitution keeping his  heart beating he's only mostly dead  fortunately the atom gets the bright  idea to solve the Batman's final case by  shrinking down and hopping around on his  brain in hopes that this will stimulate  his crime-fighting instincts and allow  him to mindlessly do things like punch  out crooks and rescue a kidnapped girl  and it actually works it turns out that  having a little tiny man hop on your  brain is enough stimulus to basically  jumpstart everything and get you back in  good working order it might not be that  great for the legend of the Batman to  have him die at random by jumping at the  wrong window but that's balanced out by  the fact that death itself cannot stop  him from getting up off a slab in the  morgue and busting criminals jaws all  over Gotham City  [Music]

